' ============================================================
' MATH - game math in pure GBX
'
'@help
'# Math
'
'Everyday game math in pure GBX: clamping and interpolation, angles,
'2D vectors, axis-aligned rectangles, collision tests, random helpers,
'value noise and curves. No C++ block, no archive, no extra files - the
'module works in the interpreter, in a built EXE and in HTML5.
'
'The module is the folder `modules/math`; `MATH.gbx` is its entry file.
'Add it with the Modules dock or write:
'
'```gbx
'INCLUDE "modules/math/MATH.gbx"
'```
'
'## Quick start
'
'```gbx
'INCLUDE "modules/math/MATH.gbx"
'
'GLOBAL pos AS VEC2
'pos.set(120, 80)
'pos.add(20, -5)
'
'GLOBAL hit AS RECT2
'hit.set(100, 60, 48, 64)
'
'IF MATH_POINT_IN_RECT(pos.x, pos.y, 100, 60, 48, 64) THEN
'  PRINT "inside", 0, 0
'ENDIF
'
'x = MATH_LERP(0, 320, 0.5)          ' 160
'a = MATH_ANGLE_LERP(350, 10, 0.5)   ' 0, the short way round
'n = MATH_NOISE2(pos.x / 64, pos.y / 64)
'```
'
'## Constants
'
'| Constant | Value |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_PI` | 3.14159265… |
'| `MATH_TAU` | 2 * PI |
'| `MATH_E` | 2.71828182… |
'| `MATH_DEG2RAD` | PI / 180 |
'| `MATH_RAD2DEG` | 180 / PI |
'| `MATH_EPS` | 0.000001, the usual "close enough" value |
'
'## Scalars
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_CLAMP(v, low, high)` | Keep `v` inside the range (the bounds may be swapped) |
'| `MATH_CLAMP01(v)` | Keep `v` inside 0…1 |
'| `MATH_LERP(a, b, t)` | `a + (b - a) * t`, `t` not clamped |
'| `MATH_LERP_CLAMPED(a, b, t)` | Same, with `t` clamped to 0…1 |
'| `MATH_INV_LERP(a, b, v)` | Where `v` lies between `a` and `b`; 0 when `a = b` |
'| `MATH_REMAP(v, inLo, inHi, outLo, outHi)` | Move a value from one range into another |
'| `MATH_REMAP_CLAMPED(...)` | Same, clamped to the output range |
'| `MATH_SMOOTHSTEP(edge0, edge1, v)` | Hermite curve from 0 to 1 with a soft start and end |
'| `MATH_SMOOTHERSTEP(edge0, edge1, v)` | Fifth-order curve, even softer |
'| `MATH_MOVE_TOWARD(current, target, maxDelta)` | Step at most `maxDelta` towards `target` |
'| `MATH_WRAP(v, low, high)` | Wrap into `[low, high)` |
'| `MATH_PINGPONG(v, length)` | Bounce back and forth between 0 and `length` |
'| `MATH_SNAP(v, step)` | Round to the nearest multiple of `step` |
'| `MATH_DEADZONE(v, dead)` | Rescale a -1…1 stick so a small centre dead zone reads 0 |
'| `MATH_DAMP(current, target, lambda, dt)` | Frame-rate independent smoothing towards `target` |
'| `MATH_DAMP_ANGLE(current, target, lambda, dt)` | Same for an angle, the short way round |
'| `MATH_NEAR(a, b [, epsilon])` | `1` when both numbers are close, `epsilon` defaults to `MATH_EPS` |
'| `MATH_SAFE_DIV(a, b [, fallback])` | `a / b`, or `fallback` (default 0) when `b` is near zero |
'| `MATH_EXP(x)` | `e` to the power of `x` (the language has `LOGN` but no `EXP`) |
'
'## Angles
'
'All angles are in degrees, like `SIN`, `COS` and `ATAN`. Because screen
'`y` points down, a positive angle turns clockwise on screen.
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_RAD(degrees)` · `MATH_DEG(radians)` | Convert between degrees and radians |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_WRAP(degrees)` | Normalize to -180…180 |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_DIFF(from, to)` | Shortest signed turn from `from` to `to` |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_LERP(from, to, t)` | Interpolate the short way round, `t` not clamped |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_LERP_CLAMPED(from, to, t)` | Same, with `t` clamped to 0…1 |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_APPROACH(current, target, maxDelta)` | Turn at most `maxDelta` degrees towards `target` |
'| `MATH_ANGLE_BETWEEN(x1, y1, x2, y2)` | Direction from the first point to the second |
'| `MATH_DIR_X(degrees)` · `MATH_DIR_Y(degrees)` | Unit direction as `COS` / `SIN` |
'
'## Vectors
'
'`VEC2` holds `x` and `y`. The methods change the vector in place and are
'called with `v.set(...)`, `v.add(...)` and so on.
'
'| Method | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `v.set(x, y)` | Assign both components |
'| `v.copyFrom(x, y)` | Copy components from another vector or plain numbers |
'| `v.add(x, y)` · `v.sub(x, y)` | Add or subtract |
'| `v.scale(factor)` | Multiply both components |
'| `v.addScaled(x, y, factor)` | Add another vector times a factor |
'| `v.length()` · `v.lengthSq()` | Length and squared length (the square is cheaper) |
'| `v.distance(x, y)` | Distance to a point |
'| `v.dot(x, y)` · `v.cross(x, y)` | Dot and 2D cross product |
'| `v.angle()` · `v.angleTo(x, y)` | Direction of the vector, and direction to a point |
'| `v.normalize()` | Make the length 1; a zero vector stays zero |
'| `v.limit(maxLength)` | Shrink to `maxLength` when longer, never grow |
'| `v.rotate(degrees)` | Rotate around the origin |
'| `v.lerpTo(x, y, t)` | Interpolate towards a point |
'
'Every `TYPE` value is a reference: `b = a` makes both names point at the
'same vector. Use `b.copyFrom(a.x, a.y)` or a fresh `LOCAL b AS VEC2` for
'an independent copy.
'
'Without an object, the same operations are available as functions:
'`MATH_LENGTH(x, y)`, `MATH_LENGTH_SQ(x, y)`,
'`MATH_DISTANCE(x1, y1, x2, y2)`, `MATH_DISTANCE_SQ(...)`,
'`MATH_DOT(x1, y1, x2, y2)`, `MATH_CROSS(x1, y1, x2, y2)`,
'`MATH_NORMAL_X(x, y)`, `MATH_NORMAL_Y(x, y)`,
'`MATH_ROTATE_X(x, y, degrees)`, `MATH_ROTATE_Y(x, y, degrees)` and
'`MATH_VEC_ANGLE(x, y)`.
'
'## Rectangles
'
'`RECT2` holds `x`, `y`, `w`, `h`; `x,y` is the top-left corner.
'
'| Method | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `r.set(x, y, w, h)` | Assign position and size |
'| `r.right()` · `r.bottom()` | Right and bottom edge |
'| `r.centerX()` · `r.centerY()` | Centre |
'| `r.area()` | `w * h` |
'| `r.contains(x, y)` | Point test, right and bottom edge exclusive |
'| `r.overlaps(x, y, w, h)` | Rectangle overlap test, touching edges are not an overlap |
'| `r.inflate(amount)` | Grow on all four sides; a negative value shrinks it |
'
'## Collision
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_POINT_IN_RECT(px, py, x, y, w, h)` | Point in a rectangle |
'| `MATH_POINT_IN_CIRCLE(px, py, cx, cy, r)` | Point in a circle |
'| `MATH_RECT_OVERLAP(x1, y1, w1, h1, x2, y2, w2, h2)` | Two axis-aligned rectangles |
'| `MATH_CIRCLE_OVERLAP(x1, y1, r1, x2, y2, r2)` | Two circles; touching counts |
'| `MATH_CIRCLE_RECT_OVERLAP(cx, cy, r, x, y, w, h)` | Circle and rectangle |
'| `MATH_RECT_OVERLAP_X(...)` | Penetration along x for two overlapping rectangles |
'| `MATH_RECT_OVERLAP_Y(...)` | Penetration along y |
'| `MATH_SEGMENT_INTERSECT(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy, dx, dy)` | Two line segments |
'| `MATH_POINT_ON_SEGMENT(px, py, x1, y1, x2, y2 [, epsilon])` | Point on a segment |
'
'`MATH_RECT_OVERLAP_X` returns `0` when the rectangles do not overlap.
'Otherwise a positive result means the first rectangle has to move right
'to separate, a negative one means left. `MATH_RECT_OVERLAP_Y` works the
'same way with positive meaning down. This is the usual way to resolve a
'platformer collision along one axis at a time:
'
'```gbx
'push = MATH_RECT_OVERLAP_X(px, py, pw, ph, tx, ty, tw, th)
'IF push <> 0 THEN px = px + push
'```
'
'## Random
'
'These helpers use the engine's generator, so `RND_SEED` makes a sequence
'reproducible.
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_RAND()` | Decimal number from 0 to 1 |
'| `MATH_RAND_RANGE(low, high)` | Decimal number in the range |
'| `MATH_RAND_INT(low, high)` | Whole number, both limits included |
'| `MATH_RAND_CHANCE(p)` | `1` with probability `p` (0…1) |
'| `MATH_RAND_SIGN()` | `-1` or `1` |
'| `MATH_RAND_INDEX(count)` | Random array index `0…count-1`, or `-1` for an empty array |
'| `MATH_RAND_ANGLE()` | Random angle from -180 to 180 |
'| `MATH_RAND_UNIT()` | Random number from -1 to 1 |
'| `MATH_RAND_SPREAD(amount)` | Random number from `-amount` to `amount` |
'
'## Noise
'
'Value noise is deterministic: a coordinate always returns the same value,
'so terrain, clouds and screenshake stay stable between frames. Results
'lie in 0…1.
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_HASH(seed)` | Deterministic pseudo random number from a seed |
'| `MATH_HASH1(ix [, seed])` | Hash of an integer lattice coordinate |
'| `MATH_HASH2(ix, iy [, seed])` | Hash of a 2D integer lattice coordinate |
'| `MATH_NOISE(x [, seed])` | Smooth 1D value noise |
'| `MATH_NOISE2(x, y [, seed])` | Smooth 2D value noise |
'| `MATH_FBM(x [, octaves [, seed]])` | Layered 1D noise, 4 octaves by default |
'| `MATH_FBM2(x, y [, octaves [, lacunarity [, gain [, seed]]]])` | Layered 2D noise |
'
'```gbx
'height = MATH_FBM2(wx / 96, 3.5) * 180
'shake = MATH_RAND_SPREAD(6)
'```
'
'## Curves
'
'| Function | Meaning |
'| --- | --- |
'| `MATH_BEZIER2(a, b, c, t)` | Quadratic Bezier between numbers |
'| `MATH_BEZIER3(a, b, c, d, t)` | Cubic Bezier between numbers |
'| `MATH_CATMULL_ROM(a, b, c, d, t)` | Smooth spline through `b` and `c` |
'| `MATH_SPRING(BYREF value, BYREF velocity, target [, stiffness [, damping [, dt]]])` | Spring step, updates value and velocity |
'| `MATH_SPRING_ANGLE(BYREF value, BYREF velocity, target [...] )` | Spring step for an angle |
'
'Evaluate a Bezier once per axis to move a point along a curve. The spring
'uses semi-implicit Euler and is frame-rate independent when `dt` is
'`DELTA_TIME() / 1000.0`:
'
'```gbx
'GLOBAL ballY = 0
'GLOBAL ballV = 0
'
'WHILE TRUE
'  MATH_SPRING(ballY, ballV, targetY, 120, 14, DELTA_TIME() / 1000.0)
'  DRAW_CIRCLE_FILLED 320, ballY, 12, RGB(56, 189, 248)
'  SCREEN_SHOW
'WEND
'```
'
'For general easing curves (smoothstep, cubic in/out, back out) the
'`EASING` module is the smaller tool; this module covers the geometry and
'gameplay side.
'
'## Notes
'
'- Everything here is pure GBX: the same file runs in the interpreter, a
'  built EXE and the HTML5 player.
'- Angles are degrees; there are no radians unless you ask with `MATH_RAD`.
'- `MATH_RECT_OVERLAP` treats touching edges as separate, while
'  `MATH_CIRCLE_OVERLAP` treats touching circles as a hit. Both choices are
'  deliberate and documented per function.
'- The noise helpers are small and fast, not a full Perlin/Simplex
'  implementation; they are meant for wobble, terrain silhouettes and
'  effects.
'@endhelp
'
'@tooltip VEC2
'A 2D vector with x and y fields. Methods: set, copyFrom, add, sub, scale, addScaled, length, lengthSq, distance, dot, cross, angle, angleTo, normalize, limit, rotate and lerpTo.
'@end
'
'@tooltip RECT2
'An axis-aligned rectangle with x, y, w, h. Methods: set, right, bottom, centerX, centerY, area, contains, overlaps and inflate.
'@end

INCLUDE "scalars.gbx"
INCLUDE "angles.gbx"
INCLUDE "vectors.gbx"
INCLUDE "collision.gbx"
INCLUDE "random.gbx"
INCLUDE "noise.gbx"
INCLUDE "curves.gbx"
